riced a C$1.1B bond comfortably, so investment-grade access looks intact.\n- **Capex plans:** Pellham plans 2027 capex up 18% and Norvane up 12%, mostly on substations and transformers.\n- **Norvane:** Norvane looks able to fund its plan through debt. It has shown market access, and its capex growth is the smaller of the two.\n- **Pellham:** Pellham withdrew its C$2.4B equity offering after demand fell short. It says it will cut 2027 transformer orders by about 40% and defer two substation programmes \"until financing improves\". That is a company choice not to lean on more debt, and it probably reflects leverage and credit-rating limits. The documents don't say this directly, so it is my inference.\n- **Equity:** Two utility share offerings were cut back in size, so equity is a weak backstop. The wider debt-funded capex has to cover more of the gap, at higher spreads.\n\n**What this means:** Debt can fund the plans in aggregate, but not all of them in full. Expect Pellham's 2027 spending to come in below plan, and expect Norvane's to be broadly on plan.\n\n**Gaps in the evidence:**\n- I found no data on debt maturity walls, leverage or credit ratings, and no figures on the size of the funding gap.\n- Norvane is the only confirmed debt deal.\n- I didn't find whether the cuts are mainly about Pellham's balance sheet or about market conditions.","ttft_ms":1521,"type":"result","duration_ms":22580,"uuid":"1b6fd0bd-6a05-4491-b521-016823ecc341","ttft_stream_ms":1279,"time_to_request_ms":77,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":5,"system_prompt":4,"resume_other":0,"process_user_input":3,"input_attachments":14,"input_hooks":2,"conversation_append":1,"transcript_persist":2,"tool_pool_refresh":0,"init_message":5,"init_frame_send":2,"engine_pickup":1,"loop_entry":3,"message_prep":1,"model_config_wait":1,"autocompact":1,"request_announcements":9,"query_setup":0,"model_gates":1,"tool_schema_build":2,"message_normalization":2,"client_creation":3,"request_build":15},"turn_start_res